new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/Backends/RdxBackend.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,1797 @@
+// rdx backend: composite straight into NIC-readable buffers for rdma-display (the gamescope path).
+//
+// Like the headless backend (no window, no DRM output), but every presented frame lands in
+// one of the output buffers that rdx_gs_src hands over a unix socket (RDX_GS_SOCKET):
+//  - VRAM: CUDA VMM allocations, linear B8G8R8A8, imported as Vulkan images from opaque
+//    fds; the NIC reads the same VRAM (peer reads through BAR1).
+//  - HOST: pinned host memory (memfds), imported with VK_EXT_external_memory_host. As a
+//    linear image if the driver allows that (the composite writes straight over PCIe),
+//    else as a buffer: composite into a VRAM image, then one GPU copy into the buffer. The
+//    NIC reads host RAM and never touches the GPU.
+// rdx_gs_src registers the memory with the RDMA NIC and writes it to the client's scanout
+// buffer.
+//
+// Pacing: rdx_gs_src frees a slot when the client returns its credit, and re-anchors our
+// vblank timer on the client's vblank grid (minus network time), so we composite just in
+// time for the client's next vblank.
+//
+// Variable refresh (RDX_GS_FLAG_VRR: the client's screen has FreeSync / VRR and shows every frame on
+// arrival): the connector reports VRR, so steamcompmgr runs gamescope's own adaptive sync path
+// (FlipType::VRR: paint as soon as the app presents, its frame limiter and flip-ready test, frame
+// callbacks at the app's pace) instead of painting on our vblank timer, and rdx_gs_src sends no
+// VBLANK. refresh_mhz is the cap (the client's maximum; the app sees it as the refresh rate). We
+// play the part of the DRM page flip: a frame "flips" when it is composited, but never sooner than
+// one cap period after the previous flip (rdx_gs_src holds the post until then, like the kernel holds
+// an early VRR flip), and that time is marked as the vblank, which is what the flip-ready test reads.
+// Below the client's minimum the client repeats frames itself. RDX_GS_VRR=0 turns it off (debugging).
+// The client's screen may switch it during the session (RDX_GS_VRR message: same buffers, new refresh).
+//
+// Resume at another client mode: rdx_gs_src sends RECONFIG (a new hello + new buffer fds).
+// The paint thread drops the old slots, imports the new ones, ACKs, and sets the new output
+// size/refresh; steamcompmgr sees g_nOutputWidth/Height change (like an SDL window resize)
+// and re-lays out. The app keeps running at its own size and is scaled to the new output.
+// READY carries the slot generation, so a late READY for an old slot is never posted.
+//
+// Stream add-on (another backend, e.g. DRM on the seat GPU's own monitor port): with
+// RDX_GS_STREAM_SOCKET set, a second instance of this class (stream mode, never the backend)
+// connects to rdx_gs_src on that path whenever one listens there (retry every 500 ms), imports
+// its slots in the paint thread and, after every frame the monitor got, composites the same
+// layers once more at the client's size into a free slot (blit shader, SDR gamma 2.2 like
+// gamescope's PipeWire stream), copies it to host RAM and sends READY. rdx_gs_src ending
+// (client gone) drops the slots; the monitor keeps running. The clock: the side of the last
+// input leads (RdxNoteInput from wlserver: devices named "rdx: ..." are rdx_input_rx's mirrors
+// of the client's devices). Monitor side: the DRM page flips drive gamescope's vblank, the
+// stream gets each frame as it comes. Client side: the client's VBLANK messages drive it, the
+// monitor follows (a frame whose flip would collide with one still in flight skips the monitor).
+//
+// Pointer hint: the connector has INestedHints like gamescope's nested backends (SDL, Wayland),
+// and steamcompmgr calls SetRelativeMouseMode( cursor hidden && pointer constrained && not Steam )
+// on it. Every change goes to rdx_gs_src as RDX_GS_HINT; it tells the client whether to send
+// relative motion (a game that locks the pointer) or absolute positions (a desktop app).
+// The stream add-on (a DRM backend, no hints) gets the same hint from RdxStreamSetRelativeMouse,
+// which steamcompmgr calls with the same rule while a stream is attached.
+//
+// The cursor next to the picture (RDX_GS_CURSOR 1 from rdx_gs_src: its client shows the cursor itself,
+// rdma-display common/rdx_cursor.h): like a nested backend, ShouldPaintCursor() is false, so steamcompmgr
+// draws no cursor into the frames and a pointer that only moves repaints nothing (wlserver_oncursorevent);
+// it hands the X cursor's picture to SetCursorImage instead (CURIMG to rdx_gs_src, in chunks), and
+// RdxCursorMoved / RdxCursorTick send where the hotspot is in output pixels (CURPOS): the focused surface's
+// position mapped like touch input in reverse (focusedWindowScale/Offset, the mapping the absolute pointer of
+// the client uses), visible = an image that is not hidden (gamescope's own hide after inactivity runs as in
+// the composited case). RDX_GS_CURSOR 0 (the start, a client without the channel) draws it again. Not in
+// stream mode: the stream add-on's frames are the monitor's and keep their cursor.
+
